<h2>Tying an Addition Into an Existing Roof</h2>
<p>The single most common problem we are called to fix on additions is not the new roof itself — it is the joint between new and old. Two roofs built years apart, often by different crews, meet at a valley or a wall, and water finds the weakest lap. A good tie-in involves more than matching shingle color:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Weaving or cutting the valley properly.</strong> Existing shingles are removed back far enough to install continuous self-adhered membrane under both roofs.</li>
  <li><strong>Step and counter-flashing at walls.</strong> Where a second story or addition wall meets a lower roof, each course gets its own step flashing, tucked behind the weather barrier.</li>
  <li><strong>Kick-out flashing.</strong> At the bottom of a roof-to-wall joint, a kick-out diverts water into the gutter instead of behind the siding or stucco.</li>
  <li><strong>Ventilation continuity.</strong> New attic space has to be vented on its own or connected to the existing system, otherwise heat and moisture build up in the addition.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Matching an older roof:</strong> shingle colors fade and product lines get discontinued. On a roof more than 10 years old, a perfect color match is rarely possible. Sometimes re-roofing the existing slopes at the same time is the better long-term value — we will show you both options. See <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">Austin roof replacement</a> for what that involves.</p></div>

<h2>Code Details We Build To</h2>
<p>Most jurisdictions in Central Texas build on versions of the International Residential Code, with local amendments. The table below summarizes common roof installation requirements and how we handle them. Always confirm the current rules with your local permitting office, since adopted code editions and amendments change.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What code generally addresses</th><th>How we install it</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Roof slope</td><td>Minimum pitch for asphalt shingles, with extra underlayment on lower slopes</td><td>We recommend metal or a low-slope membrane where pitch is too shallow for shingles</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Underlayment</td><td>Approved underlayment over the entire deck</td><td>Synthetic underlayment plus self-adhered membrane in valleys and at penetrations</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Required at eaves and rakes on shingle roofs</td><td>Metal drip edge on every eave and rake, correctly lapped with underlayment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fastening</td><td>Nail count and placement per manufacturer and wind requirements</td><td>High-wind nailing patterns, hand-checked placement</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ventilation</td><td>Minimum net free vent area based on attic size</td><td>Calculated intake and exhaust, balanced for the attic volume</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flashing</td><td>Flashing at walls, chimneys, valleys and penetrations</td><td>New step, counter, kick-out and pipe flashing on every job</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Our article on <a href="/blog/roof-replacement-permit-requirements/">roofing permit requirements</a> covers inspections and who is responsible for pulling the permit, and our <a href="/blog/roofing-underlayment-material-types/">underlayment guide</a> explains felt vs synthetic vs peel-and-stick.</p>

