<h2>Roof Installation for Austin’s Climate and Neighborhoods</h2>
<p>A roof installed in Austin has to handle attic temperatures that can climb well past 140°F in August, spring hail, and fast-moving thunderstorms with straight-line winds. That is why we put so much weight on ventilation and fastening for every Austin roof installation, and why we often suggest radiant-barrier decking and lighter-colored or reflective roofing on new builds, where those choices cost the least.</p>
<p>Terrain matters too. Homes on exposed Hill Country lots in Dripping Springs and west of MoPac take more wind than those tucked into older central neighborhoods, while new subdivisions in Leander, Hutto and Kyle often have HOA material and color rules to follow. In parts of the region with wildfire-related building requirements, roof covering fire ratings also come into play. We check the rules that apply to your address before materials are ordered. See our <a href="/service-areas/leander-tx/">Leander roofing</a> and <a href="/service-areas/dripping-springs-tx/">Dripping Springs roofing</a> pages for more local detail.</p>

<h2>Choosing Materials for a New Roof</h2>
<p>New construction is the cheapest moment to upgrade. Swapping a builder-grade shingle for a <a href="/services/shingle-roofing/">Class 4 impact-resistant composition shingle</a>, or choosing <a href="/services/metal-roofing/">standing seam metal</a>, adds cost now but avoids paying for a tear-off later. Use the calculator below to compare how long each material is likely to last in Central Texas, then request a free quote and we will price the options side by side.</p>

